{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787458175,"nonce":"IoJeF9iby1ZeVxL+S+cAH9NOBHY=","title":"The `secrets` table records nothing about WHICH master key wrapped each row, so","message":"master-key rotation is not merely unimplemented — it is unimplementable without\na schema migration.\n\nWhere — `crates/scarab-secrets-postgres/src/lib.rs:47-58`:\n\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS secrets (\n        scope       TEXT  NOT NULL,\n        key         TEXT  NOT NULL,\n        ciphertext  BYTEA NOT NULL,\n        value_nonce BYTEA NOT NULL,\n        wrapped_key BYTEA NOT NULL,\n        key_nonce   BYTEA NOT NULL,\n        PRIMARY KEY (scope, key)\n    )\n\nNo `key_id`, no `key_version`, no `wrapped_at`.\n\nThe envelope design itself is right and is exactly what makes rotation cheap in\nprinciple: each secret has its own random 256-bit data key, and only that small\ndata key is sealed under the master. Re-wrapping N data keys is cheap; the\nciphertext never moves. But you cannot re-wrap incrementally, or verify a\nrotation completed, or run two masters during a rollout, without knowing which\nmaster each row is currently under.\n\nFailure scenario: `SCARAB_MASTER_KEY` is suspected compromised. The only\navailable procedure is to decrypt every row with the old master and re-encrypt\nwith the new one in a single all-or-nothing pass, with the process holding both\nkeys, and no way to resume if it dies halfway — a half-rotated table is\nindistinguishable from a fully-rotated one, and every unrotated row fails its\nGCM auth tag with no diagnostic saying why. In practice that means the master\nkey is never rotated, which makes it a permanent credential.\n\nThis matters more than it looks because the doc comment advertises the\npluggability that rotation implies: \"the provider is pluggable (a KMS-backed\nmaster key later)\". Moving to KMS is a rotation event.\n\nFix: add `key_id TEXT NOT NULL` (and optionally `wrapped_at`), accept a set of\nmaster keys with one marked current, decrypt under the row's `key_id`, and\nre-wrap lazily on read plus a background sweep. That makes rotation resumable,\nobservable, and safe across a rolling deploy.\n\nRelated: the same file's `encrypt` binds no AAD — filed separately.","files":null}]}